  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRain showers ahead of an approaching cold front continue for\nareas north and west of the Capital District but are beginning\nto break apart and diminish. These showers may make little\nfurther eastward progression before fully dissipating. \nTherefore, most areas from the Capital District and points south\nand east will likely continue its dry spell for several more \ndays. Where it has rained, rainfall amounts up to around 0.10 \ninches have been observed with the higher amounts across the \nAdirondacks. The cold front is nearing the Adirondacks and will \ncontinue to track southeastward across the region through this \nmorning. In the wake of the front, gusty northwesterly winds \nwill develop and continue through the rest of the day with \nfrequent gusts 25 to 35 mph, strongest across the Mohawk Valley \ninto the Capital District and Berkshires. Relative humidity \nlevels will lower to 40 to 55 percent this afternoon with the \nhigher values in the higher elevations. Per collaboration with \nNYS/New England DEC's/DEP's and surrounding WFO's, given the \ndrying fuels across these areas with little or no rainfall for \nnearly 2 weeks, a Special Weather Statement has been issued \nthrough this afternoon for an elevated risk of fire spread. With\nthe cold air advection in place, temperatures will struggle to \nrise much today only reaching the mid-50s to mid-60s except \nremaining in the upper 40s to lower 50s across the Adirondacks. \nWinds will begin to diminish tonight with surface high pressure \nbuilding in from the west.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry weather is expected this weekend through the middle of next\nweek with high pressure nearby with less wind. Latest forecast\ntrends keep a backdoor frontal boundary well north of the area \nearly next week reducing precipitation chances for our area. \nHigh temperatures will start near normal this weekend, trending \nto 5 to 10 degrees above normal next week. Long range guidance \nis beginning to show signs that we finally get our much needed \nwidespread rainfall later next week with the potential \ninteraction or phasing of a deep upper level trough/upper-level \nlow approaching from the west and a low pressure system tracking\nnorthward near or just off the East Coast. Further details \nremain uncertain with this still a week out.",
